// Minimal spinlock for rare, O(1)-ish critical sections shared across the
// freestanding runtime (the scheduler's global run queue and registry, channel
// send/recv/close, the allocator's free lists). Never held across a blocking
// call or a green-thread context switch, so spinning (rather than a
// futex/condvar) is both simple and correct — and keeps the runtime free of
// libc threading primitives. One implementation so no module hand-rolls its
// own; it lives in its own file so even the lowest layer (the allocator) can
// use it without importing the scheduler.
//
// Stage-2 port of runtime/spinlock.bit (#1361). Both exist during the port;
//
// SYMBOLS. Every export is pinned with §11.9 `@symbol`, which is what makes this
// module usable as a `libbitrt.a` MEMBER rather than only as a source-level
// import. A module compiled on its own (§17.6 `--freestanding`) is the root of
// that build and emits bare names, but a module that IMPORTS it sees `m<id>$`,
// where `<id>` is an ordinal the importing build assigns — so the definition and
// the reference would be spelled differently in the two objects and never link.
// The pin is the fixed point both sides agree on. `bit_rt_spin_*` is a fresh
// name space: runtime/spinlock.bit exports nothing (it is an embedded type, absent
// from ABI.md), so nothing in the existing archive collides.
//
// SHAPE. A lock is naturally a type with methods over an embedded
// `std.atomic.Value(bool)` field. Bit's atomics (§11.5) address an integer
// through a raw `*T`, and `ptrOf` is the one bridge from traced memory to a
// raw pointer, so the Bit port is a set of free functions over a caller-owned
// lock **word** rather than a struct with methods. That is the shape the rest
// of the runtime port needs anyway: a lock lives as a word inside a larger
// runtime structure (the scheduler, an allocator arena) and is reached by
// offset, not as a value passed around.
//
// Every function here is `@nosplit` (§10.3): they are the mutual-exclusion
// machinery the allocator and collector are built on, so they must not
// allocate and must not reach a safepoint — a collection that ran while a
// worker held the allocator's lock would deadlock against the very lock it
// needs.

// Lock word states. `spinUnlocked` is also the zero value, so a lock word
// carved out of zeroed memory starts unlocked with no initialization step —
// matching a false/unlocked initial value.
//
// The word is `i32` rather than a byte: Bit's atomics require an
// integer prim (§11.5), and a 32-bit word is the conventional lock width (Go's
// runtime mutex is a `uint32`) with well-defined atomic support on both
// targets. The lock is runtime-internal — it appears nowhere in ABI.md — so the
// width is an implementation choice.
export const spinUnlocked: i32 = 0
export const spinLocked: i32 = 1

// Attempt the lock at `p` once, without spinning: true if it was free and is
// now held by the caller, false if another holder owns it.
//
// `atomicCmpxchg` succeeds only when it observed `spinUnlocked` and installed
// `spinLocked`, which is exactly the one-shot acquire. There is no
// `tryAcquire` — this is a deliberate addition, for two reasons. It is the
// operation `spinAcquire` is built from, so defining acquire in terms of it
// keeps one copy of the exclusion rule; and it makes exclusion
// DETERMINISTICALLY TESTABLE. A spin loop cannot be probed from a single
// thread (the probe would never return), and the v1 scheduler cannot produce
// true contention inside a critical section, so without this a test cannot
// tell a real lock from one that never excludes anything at all.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_spin_try_acquire") function spinTryAcquire(p: *i32): bool {
  return atomicCmpxchg(p, spinUnlocked, spinLocked)
}

// PAUSE (x64) / YIELD (arm64): the spin-wait hint issued via
// `std.atomic.spinLoopHint()` in the retry body (#1594). Pre-encoded per
// §11.6: x64 `0xF3, 0x90` (PAUSE) and arm64 the 32-bit word `0xD503203F`
// (YIELD). Neither reads nor writes any register or memory beyond signalling
// the core — a scheduling/power hint only — so the block declares no `input`,
// `result` or `clobber`. Disassembly-verified, not just built-and-linked (a
// wrong instruction word still assembles into something): `--emit-obj` +
// objdump/capstone on both architectures confirm the exact bytes/word land
// inside `spinAcquire`'s retry loop.
@nosplit function spinLoopHint() {
  asm volatile {
    x64   { 0xF3, 0x90 }
    arm64 { 0xD503203F }
  }
}

// Acquire the lock at `p`, spinning until it is won.
//
// The loop retries for exactly as long as another holder owns the word. This
// is the Go-style sense of
// `cmpxchgWeak(false, true, ...) != null` test, which spins while the swap
// FAILS (the raw primitive returns the previous value, not a bool).
//
// A spin-loop hint belongs in the retry body,
// restored above as `spinLoopHint` (#1594). The claim this file used to make —
// "`@nosplit` rejects `asm`" — is false as of §10.3.1, which admits `asm`
// precisely because its payload is emitted inline verbatim and so can neither
// allocate nor reach a safepoint (#1399). The hint is a scheduling/power
// advisory with no semantic content — it changes neither the memory ordering
// nor the outcome — so its restoration is a pure optimization, not a
// correctness fix.
//
// DEADLOCK HAZARD (v1 scheduler). `@nosplit` removes the safepoint poll, so a
// thread spinning here can never yield. Under the current cooperative,
// single-worker scheduler that means any thread descheduled while HOLDING the
// lock deadlocks every spinner permanently. Today's callers are safe only
// because their critical sections contain no safepoint. This resolves itself
// when the scheduler becomes genuinely M:N (#1364) and a spinner is a real OS
// thread making progress in parallel with the holder — which is the regime the
// this already assumes.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_spin_acquire") function spinAcquire(p: *i32) {
  let won = false
  while (!won) {
    won = spinTryAcquire(p)
    if (!won) {
      spinLoopHint()
    }
  }
}

// Release the lock at `p`. Unconditional: only the holder calls this, so there
// is nothing to compare against — a plain store.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_spin_release") function spinRelease(p: *i32) {
  atomicStore(p, spinUnlocked)
}

// The lock cell at word `slot` of the word-indexed block `block`.
//
// Every runtime structure that owns a lock is a caller-owned `*i64` block, so
// its lock lives at a WORD index while the lock itself is `i32`. Re-addressing
// the cell at its own width is the conversion every such module needs: a `*i32`
// scales by 4, so the byte address divided by 4 off the null pointer names the
// same cell. §11.4's stated inverse of pointer arithmetic, and the low half of
// the word is the one this lock touches on both targets (both little-endian).
//
// It lives here because this is the module that fixed the lock's width, so this
// is where the width and its addressing belong together. `runtime/sched` still
// carries a private twin (`lockOf`) predating this; the two should collapse into
// this one, which is deliberately NOT done in this change — #1429 is editing
// that file in parallel and a drive-by refactor of a shared file is how two
// agents produce a defect that exists only in the combination of their work.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_spin_cell_at") function spinCellAt(block: *i64, slot: int): *i32 {
  let z: *i32 = nil
  return z + int(block + slot) / 4
}
